[#4471](/kb/incidents/4471) was marked known: one issue, one fix queued. Its reports said otherwise.
Read by what each one shows, not by its subject line, they fall into three problems. Each needed
its own fix, and two of them had no fix queued at all.

<Callout>
Outcome. The desk closed #4471 on 5 October: the parser fix shipped (<code>fix(dates): parse dd/mm
for eu locales</code>), and the booking-v3 form was retired, so the <code>hint</code> reports stop
with it. The <code>shift</code> reports have no fix yet; their ticket stays open.
</Callout>

## What #4471 holds

<CauseTable ticket={4471} />

This is the desk's own table, as it was exported: each report sits under the cause its tenant
gives it, and the reports the desk read as a one-day move stay under `parser` with a footnote.
The export repeats some reports across its pages. Each report is counted once, by id.

The tickets carry the desk's counts: the `parser` ticket covers the `parser` row, the `hint`
ticket the `hint` row. The one-day reports also have a ticket of their own, because they are a
different bug; the desk's table keeps them under `parser` with the footnote.

- `parser` → <a href={issues.parser}>issue #1</a>, closed: fixed
- `hint` → <a href={issues.hint}>issue #2</a>, closed: won't fix, form retired
- `shift` → <a href={issues.shift}>issue #3</a>, open

## The three problems

**`parser`: the shared parser reads day-first dates month first.** Every tenant on file writes
the day first. A booking for 7 April comes back as 4 July: day and month swap. It reaches every
channel: app, email, and the booking-v2 form, whose hint already says `DD/MM/YYYY`. This is the
bug the queued fix is for.

**`hint`: the booking-v3 form tells people to type the month first.** Its date field prints
`MM/DD/YYYY`, so people type the month first, and the form then reads the date the tenant's way,
day first. The swap looks the same as `parser`, but it comes only through the booking-v3 form and
the parser fix does not touch it. The fix is the hint (or the reading) in booking-v3. The desk's
table marks it "not this ticket"; it has one now.

**`shift`: the date moves by one day.** These reports name the right day and month, a day apart
("Tuesday 12 May, calendar says Monday 11 May"; a client in Lisbon gets Tuesday, a client in
Toronto Monday). No day-first reading produces that. It points at time-zone handling when an
invite is built. The desk's table files these under `parser` with a footnote. They also have
a ticket of their own, as the problem they show, and that ticket is the one still open.

## How a report is placed

1. Drop repeats: one id, one report.
2. The tenant decides, as in `causes.ts`: a form report from a booking-v3 tenant is `hint`; any
   other report from a day-first tenant is `parser`. This is the desk's view, and the table above.
3. A report the desk read as a one-day move (`desk-notes.json`) also counts as `shift`. The desk's
   table keeps it where step 2 put it and footnotes it.

Intake's tag is not used. It is a guess from the reporter's browser, and the table shows how
often it is wrong.

## Why one ticket hid three problems

The desk merges on subject alone ([decision 0001](/kb/decisions/0001-dedupe-by-subject)). People
describe all three problems with the same words ("wrong day", "date is wrong"), so every report
landed in #4471 and nobody read the bodies. Decision 0001 accepted that engineering would split
tickets later; for #4471 that never happened. It needs a new decision record that replaces it;
0001 itself stays as written.

## What is not proven yet

- `hint` rests on the tenant's form, not on the report text: the bodies of `hint` and `parser`
  reports read the same. Confirm in the booking-v3 form code before the fix.
- `shift` is a small group of mixed strength. Some show the move plainly; others only say
  "one day off", "showed as Monday", or "the invite arrived before I made the booking". Treat it
  as one investigation into time-zone handling; split it if the cause turns out to differ.

